WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.420
Where did you go this time, Riptoes?

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.840
Ooh, look at those costumes.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.100
I can't sit still with this music.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.279
This show is called In the Heights.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.756
It was written by Lin-Manuel Miranda.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.560
Lin-Manuel grew up in Washington
Heights, a neighborhood in New York City.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.700
He heard salsa, merengue,
and other Latin music playing

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.406
in stores and on the streets.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.683
In high school, he acted in school plays
and began writing his own musicals, shows

00:00:30.683 --> 00:00:32.566
that tell stories through songs.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.532
In New York City, people go
to Broadway to watch musicals.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:40.570
At the time, most Broadway shows
used similar kinds of music.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.167
Miranda added hip-hop,
rap, and Latin sounds.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.980
His first musical, In the
Heights, was about people and

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.804
daily life in his neighborhood.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.280
Every year, the best Broadway shows
win prizes called Tony Awards.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.904
In the Heights won four,
including Best Musical.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.492
His next musical, Hamilton, used
hip-hop and rap to tell the story of

00:01:02.492 --> 00:01:04.080
America's Founding Fathers.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.080
The show cast actors of many different
backgrounds as the Founding Fathers,

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.374
something Broadway had not done before.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.640
Hamilton set a record with 16 Tony
nominations, the most any show had ever

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.600
received. It won 11.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.568
What do you think makes Lin-Manuel
Miranda's musicals different

00:01:22.568 --> 00:01:24.098
from other Broadway shows?

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.920
They don't sound like
regular Broadway songs.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.968
They sound like music
from his neighborhood.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.600
Today, his musicals are performed
around the world, and many

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.129
people watch them at home.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.640
I didn't expect a Broadway
show to sound like that.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.280
It sounds like a block party.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.500
He just used the music he grew up with.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.842
If you wrote a song about your
neighborhood, what would it sound like?
